laurel 2155: GPU watchdog + kern.laurel_gpu. While backboardd draws with GL, the GPU thread checks the fence every 250 ms: work pending and the written-back submission number unchanged for 3 s = a GPU hang (idle is never a hang: a still screen sends no work; RedwoodGPU polls fences, there is no GPU interrupt, so a hung core showed only as timed-out waits). Once: the TopazGPU register line, 'LAUREL GPU: off: GPU hang: submissions A-B not done, no progress for N ms; GL off, backboardd PID restarts on the CPU renderer', CP stopped and ready = false (RedwoodGPU refuses, topaz_gpu_gl_active() false: screen surfaces from ordinary memory), SIGKILL to the GL backboardd. New read-only sysctl kern.laurel_gpu = 'on' | 'off: <reason>' (gpu=0, bring-up step failed, GPU hang) | '' while the GPU starts; the backboardd wrapper (userland/laurel/gpu/bbd_wrap.c) reads it and runs backboardd without the GL loader when it is off (CPU renderer at once, no 5 s RedwoodGPU wait). Kill switch gpuwd=0. Found while checking the 2026-11-01 06:12/06:17 'GL stall' (docs/laurel_sprout/gpu.md 'GL stall'): that one was not the GPU (SpringBoard and backboardd wait for lsd), but nothing caught a real GPU hang before.

